  <hr>

  <!-- STATE -->
  <section class="section">
    <span class="section-label">Data</span>
    <h2 id="state">State Management</h2>

    <Callout type="tip" title="What is state management?">
      State is VibeCrew's <strong>memory between sessions</strong>. It remembers what features you've planned, which ones are done, your quality scores, session history, and configuration preferences. All of this is stored in a folder called <code>.vibecrew/</code> inside your project. Unlike the AI's conversation memory (which resets every session), these files persist forever. They're plain JSON files &mdash; human-readable text &mdash; so you can always open them and see exactly what VibeCrew knows about your project.
    </Callout>

    <p>All VibeCrew runtime state lives in <code>.vibecrew/</code>:</p>
    <TableWrap>
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>File / Directory</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Updated By</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>config.json</code></td><td>Terminal preference, notification settings, feature toggles, user profile</td><td>/setup, /profile</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>state.json</code></td><td>Foundation status + active feature + active workflow + phase tracking</td><td>All commands</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>backlog.json</code></td><td>Feature backlog with specs, priorities, dependencies</td><td>/plan-features, /idea, /run-backlog</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>gamification.json</code></td><td>XP, level, badges, skill tree, streaks, challenges, quiz progress</td><td>/wrap (gamification pipeline)</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>mutation-log.json</code></td><td>CLAUDE.md mutation audit log with timestamps and diffs</td><td>Performance Coach</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>sessions/*.json</code></td><td>Session logs with phase data and token metrics</td><td>/wrap</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>scores/*.json</code></td><td>Vibe Score breakdowns with deductions and bonuses</td><td>Performance Coach</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>releases/*.json</code></td><td>Release notes data</td><td>Doc Generator</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>handoffs/*.md</code></td><td>Cross-session context transfer documents</td><td>/handoff, /wrap</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>workflows/*.json</code></td><td>Reusable workflow templates from high-scoring sessions</td><td>/replay --create</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>simplifications/*.json</code></td><td>Code simplification reports with per-suggestion status</td><td>/simplify</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>ci-heals/*.json</code></td><td>CI heal reports documenting diagnosis and fix attempts</td><td>/heal</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </TableWrap>

    <h4>Git strategy for .vibecrew/</h4>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Solo developer:</strong> Commit everything &mdash; preserves session history across machines</li>
      <li><strong>Teams:</strong> Commit <code>state.json</code>, <code>backlog.json</code>, and <code>scores/</code>. Gitignore <code>config.json</code> (terminal-specific)</li>
      <li><strong>Sensitive projects:</strong> Add <code>.vibecrew/</code> to <code>.gitignore</code> entirely</li>
    </ul>

    <h4>State schema migration</h4>
    <p>When VibeCrew is upgraded, <code>scripts/migrate-state.sh</code> automatically detects the current schema version and applies incremental migrations (1.0.0 &rarr; 1.1.0 &rarr; 1.2.0 &rarr; 1.3.0). New fields are added with sensible defaults. No manual migration is needed.</p>
  </section>

</DocsLayout>
